; Regression fixture for json-gen extern schema rows (feature request 05). The json-gen crate must
; NOT emit a `gen_json_schema!(cddl_lib::ExtSet)` row for the generic-extern BASE (bare `ExtSet`
; names no concrete type — E0107), while KEEPING the plain extern (`MyExtern`) and the concrete
; generic INSTANCE (`MySet = ExtSet<u64>`) rows, which compile because the user's hand-written
; externs implement `schemars::JsonSchema` (the documented `--json-schema-export` extern contract).
;
; `@no_json_schema_export` compile proof (the `no_schema_extern` / `hand_json_parent` pair). An
; own-spec extern whose hand-written rust type deliberately has NO `schemars::JsonSchema` impl —
; because its published JSON encoding is produced by its PARENT's hand-written impl, not by a
; derive on itself. Without the directive its `gen_json_schema!` row is an `E0277` INSIDE a
; generated file (the one file class a consumer is told never to hand-edit); with it, the json-gen
; crate this fixture builds compiles. The parent carries `@custom_json @no_json_schema_export`
; together — the legal, meaningful combination ("I supply the JSON impls, and this type is not a
; published schema root") — which is also what keeps the parent from deriving `schemars::JsonSchema`
; over a field type that has none.
my_extern = _CDDL_CODEGEN_EXTERN_TYPE_
ext_set<T> = _CDDL_CODEGEN_EXTERN_TYPE_
my_set = ext_set<uint>
no_schema_extern = _CDDL_CODEGEN_EXTERN_TYPE_ ; @no_json_schema_export
hand_json_parent = [n: no_schema_extern] ; @custom_json @no_json_schema_export
big_thing = [e: my_extern, s: my_set]

; Green-direction vector for the emitted document's reference-CLOSURE check, whose helper must DECODE
; a `$ref` before matching it against a `$defs` key. `EncodedName`'s hand-written `schema_name()` is
; `Odd<K>/~1café`, which schemars spells in a `$ref` as `Odd%3CK%3E~1~01caf%C3%A9` — one name covering
; all three escape classes at once (percent for `<`/`>` and multi-byte `é`, JSON-Pointer `~1` for
; `/`, `~0` for `~`) AND the decode's ORDER, which the three classes alone cannot witness: the
; literal `~1` in the NAME encodes to `~01`, so unescaping `~0` before `~1` yields `Odd<K>//café`
; while the correct order yields the name back.
; `encoded_holder`
; exists so something REFERENCES it: a root's own ref is discarded, so without a referrer no encoded
; `$ref` would appear in the document at all. A decode that is wrong in any of the three classes
; fails this fixture's json-gen run, which is the only place the decode is load-bearing.
encoded_name = _CDDL_CODEGEN_EXTERN_TYPE_
encoded_holder = [e: encoded_name]

; A `@no_json_schema_export` extern as an open struct-map rest row's KEY domain, under
; --json-schema-export. The rest row's flattened region publishes a K-FREE open-object schema, so the
; key's missing `schemars::JsonSchema` impl is not an E0277 here — which is the whole reason that
; helper asks nothing of K. (The parent still derives `JsonSchema`: the rest field is answered by the
; `schema_with` annotation, so the derive never asks the field type for one.)
no_schema_key_rest = { 1: uint, * no_schema_extern => uint }
